        // But share is address(this), so we define a fallback in this contract (but Test already has a fallback? No, it doesn't). We'll define a receive/fallback to simulate.
        // However, the buggy version's allowance will call address(c).share.call(msg.data), and if msg.sender is the test contract, it will call this contract's fallback. We'll make fallback call c.updatePrice(1) which is allowed in buggy but reverts in reference.
        // Actually the reference's require(wards[msg.sender]==1) will fail, causing revert. The buggy passes. So allowance will revert on reference, succeed on buggy.
        // So we can just call allowance and see if it reverts or not.

        // Setup: grant auth to this test contract on c (so we can call updatePrice directly if needed, but the test uses allowance)
        // c.rely(address(this)); // This would require auth itself, which we don't have initially. But in constructor, msg.sender (this test contract) is given ward=1, so c.rely(address(this)) works?
